Our greatest advantage as humans isn’t that we’re the strongest, fastest, or toughest animal.

It’s that we can change the conditions of existence instead of merely competing within them.

A bear has tremendous strength. A tiger has extraordinary speed and predatory instincts. An eagle has exceptional eyesight.

But humans can study all three, learn their advantages, invent tools that reproduce or exceed particular capabilities, and then teach those discoveries to people who haven’t even been born yet.

That is an extraordinary evolutionary advantage.

And I think some of our greatest advantages are considerably more subtle than intelligence alone.

The human advantage stack

1. Precision, throwing, and toolmaking

Human shoulders can store and release elastic energy to produce exceptionally fast, accurate throws. Combined with our dexterous hands, this allows us to turn ordinary materials into tools that extend our reach, force, and precision.

2. Endurance and thermoregulation

A tiger can sprint much faster than a human. But people are well adapted for sustained movement, particularly in conditions where evaporative cooling works well. Sweating, efficient walking, and endurance running allow us to operate over time and distance.

3. Cooperation and specialization

One human is vulnerable. Twenty humans communicating, dividing responsibilities, and sharing resources are an entirely different proposition.

We can organize specialists, coordinate with unrelated people, and build institutions that make cooperation possible far beyond a small group.

4. Language and external memory

We can describe things that aren’t present, teach complex procedures, communicate hypothetical dangers, and preserve knowledge outside our brains.

Writing, diagrams, books, software, and now AI allow one person’s discoveries to become resources for millions.

5. Cumulative culture

This is arguably our greatest advantage.

An individual doesn’t need to invent metallurgy, mathematics, agriculture, or medicine. They can inherit that knowledge and improve it.

Human culture can accumulate capabilities across generations that no single individual could develop independently.

The deeper, less obvious advantages

These are the ones I find especially interesting.

Capability	What it allows us to do
Mental simulation	Rehearse possible futures without experiencing every mistake firsthand
Abstraction	Recognize the same underlying pattern in a hunt, a military operation, a business, or a game
Cross-domain transfer	Borrow an effective mechanism from one field and repurpose it in another
Recursive improvement	Improve not just a tool, but the method used to invent better tools
Environmental engineering	Build shelter, agriculture, defenses, cities, and climate-controlled environments
Delayed gratification	Sacrifice a small immediate reward for a larger future advantage
Social intelligence	Build trust, negotiate, form alliances, teach, and reconcile competing interests
Adaptability	Switch strategies when the environment changes rather than relying on one fixed specialization

Some of these abilities exist in other species too. What distinguishes humanity is the extraordinary flexibility, scale, and cumulative interaction of the whole system.

Here’s where it gets really interesting

Think about the same tiger encountering human beings at different stages of technology.

